{
 "id": 359,
 "created_at": "2026-11-21T10:48:59.729157+05:30",
 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DfTEstrtmKV/",
 "title": "The Paradox of Getting Better",
 "topic": "mindset",
 "summary": "The creator explains that people often misinterpret the struggle of learning as proof of inability rather than part of the growth process. The main lesson is that experiencing difficulty and weakness is necessary feedback for skill development rather than a verdict on potential.",
 "key_points": [
  "Before becoming more skilled, you must experience how unskilled you are.",
  "Before becoming stronger, you must confront how weak you are.",
  "Before becoming successful, you may have to experience failure repeatedly.",
  "Two people can struggle with the same skill, but one sees an opportunity to improve while the other questions their capability.",
  "Struggling with something you are trying to learn is not proof you cannot do it, but evidence that you haven't learned how yet.",
  "Mistaking the discomfort of development for evidence that development isn't happening causes motivation to disappear.",
  "The creator spent decades developing basketball players and learned that acquiring understanding can be more important than developing the skill.",
  "The greatest obstacle to getting better is often what you've convinced yourself your struggle means."
 ],
 "content": "Discovering what you can’t do isn’t proof that you can’t do it. It’s an opportunity to understand what you need to develop. Don’t mistake the difficulty of becoming better for evidence that you can’t become better. ❤️🎯 #basketballtruth #basketballtraining #basketballandmotivation #playerdevelopment #RIPCandy Hey, what if the reason you're losing confidence is that you don't understand what getting better actually looks like? Now think about that. Sometimes before you can become more skilled, you have to experience just how unskilled you are. Before you can become stronger, you have to confront how weak you are. And before you can become successful at something, you may have to experience failure over and over again. You see, there's a paradox to getting better. There's a paradox to life. And one of the most fascinating things that I've witnessed is how differently people interpret the exact same experience. Two people can struggle with the same skill. One sees an opportunity to improve. The other begins questioning whether they're capable of improving at all. Same struggle, different interpretation. And here's what always amazed me. I can often see what people were capable of becoming long before they could see it themselves, only because I understood something they hadn't learned yet. Struggling with something you're trying to learn isn't necessarily evidence that you can't do it. Sometimes it's simply evidence that you haven't learned how to do it yet. But when you misunderstand that distinction, something dangerous happens. You begin interpreting the discomfort of development as evidence that development isn't happening. And now your motivation starts disappearing, not because you don't wanna succeed, but because you have misunderstood what the process is trying to tell you. Listen to me. I spent decades developing basketball players and one thing that experience has taught me is that acquiring the understanding can be more important than developing the skill. Because discovering what you can't do isn't proof that you can't do it. It's information about what you need to develop. And when you understand what improvement actually requires, you stop allowing every difficult experience to become a verdict on your potential. You start recognizing it as part of the process. Sometimes the greatest obstacle to getting better isn't what you can't do. It's what you've convinced yourself your struggle means. Hey, I spent decades having these conversations with players, usually behind closed doors. Now I'm having them right here right now with you. So follow me because there's a lot more we need to talk about. And don't forget to let us know where you're grinding in this world. You know how much we love knowing where you're receiving our message.",
